{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And all who went up with him.</b> He hints here at the fierce battle they had over the burial of Jacob, and therefore he needed to explain that he and his brothers and all who went up with him returned in peace — not a man of them was missing, neither in that fierce battle nor on the road, for the merit of the righteous stood them in good stead while they were occupied with him. And so Scripture hints at this matter in the episode of the battle of Shechem, for there Jacob needed to gird his weapons of war against them, as the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, darshaned: had he not done so, Simeon and Levi would have been killed. Therefore he needed to explain (Genesis 35:6): ״ויבא יעקב לוזה אשר בארץ כנען״ — 'And Jacob came to Luz, which is in the land of Canaan' — and he added the words ״הוא וכל העם אשר עמו״ — 'he and all the people who were with him' — intending to say that not a man of them was missing in that mighty battle." "<b>After he buried his father.</b> What was the need for Scripture to specify Joseph in the matter of the burial? For it already mentioned above ״ויעל יוסף לקבור את אביו״, and it further says ״וישאו אותו בניו״ — and behold, Joseph is included among them, for he is the master, the leader, and the greatest of them all." "<b>But</b> it is possible to interpret that he needed to specify and mention 'after he buried his father' so that no person would have doubts about the oath of Joseph, and therefore he hung the matter of the burial on Joseph alone, as if the rest of the sons were not there. From this he said אחרי קברו את אביו — 'after he buried his father' — and did not say אחרי קברם — 'after they buried.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:00.647243109Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}